---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="Search - Harbor Legal Independent Index" description="Search coverage and source monitoring plans for Harbor Legal Independent Index." activeNav="search">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Search</p>
      <h1>Search legal sources, not just published reports.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The search surface is where Harbor Legal Independent Index grows from a report site into a
        legal-source intelligence product. The first layer is American laws and bill systems across
        federal and state jurisdictions. Court material is not the main scope unless it is circuit
        court level or above.
      </p>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag">Federal bills</span>
        <span class="tag">State legislatures</span>
        <span class="tag">Endpoint health</span>
        <span class="tag">Schema drift</span>
        <span class="tag">Higher courts only later</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">What search will cover</div>
          <div class="card-value">Sources</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Official state legislature sites, federal bill systems, search portals, feeds, static
            pages, file exports, and any published metadata endpoint that can support bill tracking.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">What search will show</div>
          <div class="card-value">Status</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Whether a source has a working scraper, needs browser rendering, has blocking behavior,
            changed design, or is stable enough to graduate into long-term collection.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">What search leads to</div>
          <div class="card-value">Analytics</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Coverage maps, trend views, stage-change dashboards, endpoint drift alerts, and later a
            paid workflow for saved searches and member-only monitoring.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Multi-tier collection</p>
          <h2>How source gathering should mature over time</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="roadmap-grid">
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 1</div>
          <h3>Discovery and viability checks</h3>
          <p>Start by locating official endpoints, search pages, feeds, and export patterns. The first question is whether a scraper works at all and how fragile the source appears.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 2</div>
          <h3>Direct structured collection</h3>
          <p>Prefer official JSON, XML, CSV, RSS, or predictable HTML over anything expensive. If a direct collector works, that becomes the cheap default lane.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 3</div>
          <h3>Browser or container fallback</h3>
          <p>When sites require client rendering, complex pagination, or session state, move the source into a Playwright-style browser worker or an isolated container collector.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 4</div>
          <h3>Unblocking services only when needed</h3>
          <p>Use Bright Data or similar tools only for sources that block normal collection. That keeps cost low while still giving you a path through anti-bot friction.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 5</div>
          <h3>Drift detection and endpoint health</h3>
          <p>Track DOM signatures, response shapes, screenshots, status codes, and schema checksums so source redesigns are detected before they silently poison the data pipeline.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="roadmap-step">
          <div class="card-label">Tier 6</div>
          <h3>Promotion into durable pipelines</h3>
          <p>Once a source is stable, move it into scheduled collection, normalized schemas, and production monitoring with alerting and retry logic.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure options</p>
          <h2>How this can be operated without locking into one shape too early</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="signal-stack">
        <div class="signal-item">A local machine at home works well for low-cost source discovery, schema experiments, and less frequent validation runs.</div>
        <div class="signal-item">One or more containers are a good fit when you want isolated browser collectors, per-state workers, or reproducible scraping environments.</div>
        <div class="signal-item">A small VPS is useful when you need always-on schedulers, endpoint health checks, or public API jobs without leaving a home machine running.</div>
        <div class="signal-item">A hybrid model is likely best: direct collectors run cheaply most of the time, while browser or Bright Data lanes are used only on harder jurisdictions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="surface">
        <p class="eyebrow">Membership later</p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 0.6rem">The likely product path after search is working</h2>
        <p class="lead" style="margin:0; max-width:unset">
          Once source search, monitoring, and trends are stable, the next step is gated access:
          accounts, saved searches, source watchlists, alerts, and paid membership for deeper legal
          intelligence views. That should come after source quality and pipeline reliability are
          proven, not before.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
